def copy_tiers_to_versions(apps, schema_editor):
    """Every (habit, tier) that exists as a HabitTier OR is referenced by a log /
    placement -> a contiguous Version row (level 1..N, low tier first), value = the
    HabitTier's newest TierValue (blank if none), label = the tier.

    Why the UNION and not just HabitTier: a habit can have historical logs at a
    tier it no longer keeps a HabitTier for (e.g. "Outfit" was un-tiered but its
    old Roots/Growth completions remain). Copying only current HabitTiers would
    leave those logs unmappable, and P1's version-keyed uniqueness would then see
    two "no rung" logs on one day and refuse. Minting a rung for every tier the
    data actually uses keeps all history and makes P1 apply cleanly. Such a
    recovered rung has a blank value; she can delete it later (logs SET_NULL).

    Renumbering to 1..N (not the global tier level) keeps `level` meaning "position
    on THIS ladder"; the tag lives on `label`, so P1 maps old tier-keyed rows onto
    versions by label and a later relabel never disturbs history.
    """
